1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,110
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:10,190
Today, we’re exploring the sunk cost fallacy, a cognitive bias that often leads to irrational decision-making.

3
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:14,760
The sunk cost fallacy occurs when individuals continue an endeavor

4
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:19,350
or consume a product as a result of previously invested resources, such as money,

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:20,131
time,

6
00:00:20,131 --> 00:00:24,260
or effort, even when the current costs outweigh the future benefits.

7
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:30,040
Essentially, people feel that they shouldn't "waste" what they've already spent.

8
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:38,542
For instance, imagine you bought a ticket to a three-hour movie.After thirty minutes, you realize the movie is terrible

9
00:00:38,542 --> 00:00:39,515
and you aren't enjoying it.

10
00:00:39,515 --> 00:00:45,200
However, most people will stay until the end because they already paid for the ticket.

11
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:51,433
In reality, the money is gone—it’s a "sunk cost"—and by staying, they are only

12
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:54,000
wasting two more hours of their time.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,080
In business, this fallacy can be even more damaging.

14
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:03,531
Companies might continue to fund a failing project just because they have already invested

15
00:01:03,531 --> 00:01:07,425
millions of dollars into it, rather than cutting their losses

16
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:10,150
and redirecting resources to more profitable ventures.

17
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:14,200
Understanding this bias is crucial for making objective choices.

18
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:22,030
Next, we will discuss how to recognize and avoid the sunk cost fallacy in long-term financial planning.
